## ABSTRACT
It has been of interest in seeking electrocatalysts that could exercise equally high-efficient and durable hydrogen evolution upon nonselective electrolytes in both acidic and alkaline environments. Herein, we report a facile strategy to fabricate cobalt tungsten phosphides $(\mathsf{C o}_{x}\mathsf{W}_{2-x}\mathsf{P}_{2}/\mathsf{C})$ hollow polyhedrons with tunable composition based on metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) template method. By the deliberate control of W doping, the synthesized catalyst with the composition of $\mathsf{C o}_{0.9}\mathsf{W}_{1.1}\mathsf{P}_{2}/\mathsf{C}$ is found to be able to achieve a current density of $10\mathsf{m A}{\cdot}\mathsf{c m}^{-2}$ at overpotentials of 35 and $54~\mathsf{m V}$ in acidic and alkaline media, respectively. This combined electrochemical property stands atop the state-of-the-art electrocatalyst counterparts. To unveil the peculiar behavior of the structure, density functional theory (DFT) calculation was implemented and reveals that the surface W-doping facilitates the optimization of hydrogen absorption free energy $(\Delta G_{H^{\prime}})$ as well as the thermodynamic and kinetics barriers for water dissociation, which is coupled with the hollow structure of Co-W phosphides, leading to the prominent HER catalytic performance.
## KEYWORDS
cobalt tungsten phosphide, $\boldsymbol{\mathsf{W}}$ -doping, tunable composition, hydrogen evolution reaction, acid and alkaline media
## Introduction
Developing high-efficiency and robust electrocatalysts to facilitate hydrogen production is a significant solution to cope with both energy and climate crisis [1]. Hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) is a key step for electrochemical water splitting to produce high-purity hydrogen [2]. Pt-based catalysts have been proved to be capable of significantly improving the electron-to-hydrogen conversion efficiency for HER [3]. However, high cost and scarcity of $\mathrm{Pt}$ are the main issues to limit its wide applications. As a consequence, the development of highefficiency $\mathrm{{Pt}}$ -substitutes from earth-abundant elements has drawn great attention. Recently, various transition metal-based alloys, oxides, sulfides, phosphides and selenides are all potential active electrocatalysts for HER [4–11]. Among them, transition metal phosphides (TMPs) are regarded as the most promising ones due to their hydrogenase-like catalytic mechanism [12–14].
To further increase the intrinsic activity of certain TMPs, doping another element into TMPs has been widely reported to improve their HER catalytic activities by tuning their surface electronic structures to optimize the hydrogen absorption properties [15–18]. It is demonstrated that the incorporation of Mo element into cobalt or nickel phosphides is an effective approach to boost their HER activity via electronic structure modulation [19–21]. Similar to Mo, W could also lead to the downshift in energy level of $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{e_{g}}}$ orbitals attributing to its strong electron-donating capacity, which is beneficial to enhance the hydrogen absorption [22–24]. However, traditional methods by introducing W into 3d transition metal phosphides (CoP, $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{P},$ NiP, FeP, etc.) W is challenging due to the phase-separation tendency [25]. To address this concern, several ternary TMPs containing 3d transition metal and W (Mo) element have been successfully fabricated with remarkable HER catalytic activity [26–28]. Furthermore, the composition tuning in ternary TMPs is more accessible than binary TMPs ascribed to the higher complexity, which makes them promising candidates as HER electrocatalysts.
In addition, extensive efforts have been made to promote the catalytic performance by structural engineering in order to creating more active sites and efficient channels for electron transfer in a robust frame with a high surface to volume ratio, which could facilitate a better catalytic kinetics and stability [29–31]. Metal organic frameworks (MOFs) are known as perfect sacrificial templates and metal precursors to prepare well-defined hollow structures. Variety of MOF-derived species have been developed with precisely control at molecular scale and successfully applied in many fields owing to their fast mass transport, excellent conductivity, high surface area and so on [32–37]. Moreover, MOFs precursors can serve as a carbon source to further promote the electronic conductivity and improve the free energy of hydrogen absorption.
## 2 Results and discussion
The morphology and structure of the as-fabricated Co-W precursors were characterized by electron microscopic technique. It is seen that the surface of Co-W precursor polyhedrons are constructed with numerous interconnected nanosheets (Fig. S2 in the ESM), and the partial destroyed shell demonstrates their hollow structure. Scanning transmission electron microscopyenergy-dispersive $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray spectroscopy (STEM-EDX) mapping suggests that elements C, O, Co and W are homogeneously distributed across the hollow Co-W precursor particle and the nanosheets (Fig. S3 in the ESM). After the pyrolysis process, the overall morphology of Co-W precursor polyhedrons are spotted to be well preserved except the slight shrinkage of the nanosheets during the phosphorization process (Fig. 2(a)). The uniform distribution of $\mathrm{\DeltaP}$ element instead of O element over the $\mathrm{Co_{0.9}W_{1.1}P_{2}/C}$ polyhedron particle (Fig. 2(b)) proved by STEM-EDX mapping indicates the successful conversion of Co-W precursor to cobalt tungsten phosphide. The highresolution TEM (HRTEM) image that was taken from a singlecrystalline nanosheet clearly reveals the coverage of a thin amorphous carbon layer over the surface of $\mathrm{Co_{0.9}W_{1.1}P_{2}/C}$ polyhedron (Fig. 2(c)). In addition, the fast fourier transform (FFT) pattern derived from the crystalline nanosheet matches that of ${\mathrm{CoWP}}_{2}$ (ICSD 01-081-4849) in the projection plane of (001). X-ray diffraction patterns in Fig. 2(d) demonstrate that three Co-W phosphides present identical ${\mathrm{CoWP}}_{2}$ crystalline structure. Due to the increase of W to $\scriptstyle\mathrm{Co}$ atomic ratio and the larger atomic radius of W than Co, the substitution of Co atoms by W atoms within $\mathrm{Co_{0.9}W_{1.1}P_{2}/C}$ brings about the lattice expansion leading to the slight shift of the diffracted peaks towards the low angle direction. This is further confirmed by the $d$ -spacing of (100) lattice plane of $\mathrm{Co_{0.9}W_{1.1}P_{2}/C}$ polyhedron, which is measured to be $0.288\mathrm{nm}$ which is slightly larger than $0.283~\mathrm{nm}$ of the standard ${\mathrm{CoWP}}_{2}$ quoted from the database record. The specific surface area and porosity of $\mathrm{Co}_{0.95}\mathrm{W}_{1.05}\mathrm{P}_{2}/$ C were measured by the Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) and
Barrett–Joyner–Halenda (BJH) method, respectively. The $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ adsorption–desorption isotherms (Fig. S5(a) in the ESM) and the pore size distribution curves (Fig. S5(b) in the ESM) suggest a $32.5~\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ BET surface area and a mesoporous structure, respectively. The higher surface area is expected to provide more catalytic active sites while the mesoporous structure could faciitate the rapid charge and mass transport.
The compositions of carbon incorporated Co-W phosphides were further perfromed by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS), as shown in Fig. 3. The ICP-AES results indicate that the finely controlled solvothermol reaction followed by the phosphorization process successfully produced three cobalt tungsten phosphides with the $\mathrm{Co}/\mathrm{W}$ ratio of 1:1, 0.95:1.05 and 0.9:1.1, respectively (Fig. S4 in the ESM). The high-resolution C 1s spectrum (Fig. 3(a)) consists of a strong peak of C–C species $(284.5\ \mathrm{eV})$ and two relatively weak peaks of C–O–C $(286.0\ \mathrm{~eV})$ and $0-C=0$ $(288.4~\mathrm{~eV})$ , which confirms the incorporation of carbon into Co-W phosphides [38]. The $C{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{O}$ bonds are attributed to the adsorption of oxygen onto the Co-W-P/C polyhedrons surface. In the high resolution spectra of $\mathrm{Co}~2\mathrm{p}$ (Fig. 3(b)), two spin-orbit doublets are characterized as ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ and ${\mathrm{Co}}^{3+}$ , coupled with two shake-up satellite peaks [39]. The W 4f spectra could also be deconvoluted into $\mathrm{W}^{5+}$ and $\mathrm{W}^{6+}$ two spin-orbit doublets [40], as shown in Fig. 3(c). Two distinct peaks at 129.4 and $130.2\mathrm{eV}$ (Fig. 3(d)) can be allocated to the P $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ and $\mathrm{~P~}2\mathsf{p}_{1/2},$ respectively, and another peak at $133.9\ \mathrm{eV}$ could be assigned to the surface oxidized phosphorus species as a result of air exposure. Raman spectrum of $\mathrm{Co}_{0.95}\mathrm{W}_{1.05}\mathrm{P}_{2}/\mathrm{C}$ (Fig. S6 in the ESM) contains two broad peaks located at 1,398 and $1,582\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , which are associated with typical $\mathrm{~D~}$ and G bands of carbon, respectively [41]. The small $I_{\mathrm{D}}/I_{\mathrm{G}}$ ratio indicates a high degree of graphitization in $\mathrm{Co}_{0.95}\mathrm{W}_{1.05}\mathrm{P}_{2}/\mathrm{C},$ which renders $\mathrm{Co}_{0.95}\mathrm{W}_{1.05}\mathrm{P}_{2}/\mathrm{C}$ having a good electronic conduction.
The HER property of $\mathrm{Co_{0.9}W_{1.1}P_{2}/C}$ was first tested using a three-electrode system in $0.5\:\mathrm{M}\:\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ solution. For comparison, three catalysts with similar structure, ${\mathrm{CoWP}}_{2}/\mathrm{C},$ $\mathrm{Co_{0.95}W_{1.05}P_{2}/C}$ and ${\mathrm{CoP/C}},$ decorated on CC were also measured. From the LSV curves in Figs. 4(a) and $4(\mathrm{b})$ , the $\mathrm{Co_{0.9}W_{1.1}P_{2}/C}$ exhibits only a small overpotential of $35~\mathrm{mV}$ to reach the current density of $10\mathrm{\mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , which is only $10~\mathrm{mV}$ higher than $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ $(25~\mathrm{mV})$ . Moreover, this value is lower than those of $\mathrm{Co}_{0.95}\mathrm{W}_{1.05}\mathrm{P}_{2}/\mathrm{C}$ $(59~\mathrm{mV})$ and ${\mathrm{CoWP}}_{2}/{\mathrm{C}}$ $(76~\mathrm{mV})$ ), implying the controlled W doping is an effective approach to boost the HER activity of ${\mathrm{CoWP}}_{2}$ in acid solution. According to Fig. 4(c), $\mathrm{Co_{0.9}W_{1.1}P_{2}/C}$ exhibits the smallest Tafel slope $(34~\mathrm{mV\cdotdec^{-1}}),$ ) among all the Co-W phosphide catalysts, which is also comparable to that of $\mathrm{Pt/C},$ , indicating its excellent HER reaction kinetics [42]. Furthermore, electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) was also employed to study their catalytic kinetics under HER operating conditions. As depicted in Fig. 4(d), the Nyquist plots of all catalysts present semicircular portion, where the radius of the semicircle in the high-frequency range is proportional to the charge-transfer resistance $\mathrm{R_{ct}}$ . Among the phosphide catalysts, the smallest radius of $\mathrm{Co_{0.9}W_{1.1}P_{2}/C}$ suggests its smallest $\mathrm{R_{ct}}$ value (i.e. the fastest electrode reduction reaction rate) during the HER process, which is also in agreement with the linear sweep results above. In order to further investigate the enhanced electrochemical performance beneficial from composition tuning, electrochemical double layer capacitance $\left(C_{\mathrm{dl}}\right)$ of three Co-W phosphide catalysts is measured to estimate their electrochemically active surface area (ECSA). As illustrated in Fig. S7 in the ESM, the calculated $C_{\mathrm{dl}}$ of $\mathrm{Co_{0.9}W_{1.1}P_{2}/C}$ is $30.2\mathrm{mF}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , which is much higher than those of $\mathrm{Co}_{0.95}\mathrm{W}_{1.05}\mathrm{P}_{2}/\mathrm{C}$ $(22.8~\mathrm{mF}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2})$ and ${\mathrm{CoWP}}_{2}/{\mathrm{C}}$ ( $14.7~\mathrm{mF{\cdot}c m^{-2}},$ ). The large $\mathrm{C_{dl}}$ of $\mathrm{Co_{0.9}W_{1.1}P_{2}/C}$ is attributed to the abundant active sites on interconnected nanosheets controlled by tuning its atomic structure, achieving a very large ECSA. Moreover, the unique hollow structure that is full of macro-/mesopores is beneficial for fast mass transport as well as charge transfer.
More importantly, the $\mathrm{Co_{0.9}W_{1.1}P_{2}/C}$ shows promising longterm stability (Fig. 4(f)) in acid solution after subject to a constant current density of $20\mathrm{\mF}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ for $50\mathrm{~h~}$ . The LSV profile after long-term electrolysis also illustrates a negligible voltage decrease, indicating the superb stability of $\mathrm{Co_{0.9}W_{1.1}P_{2}/C}$ in acid solution (Fig. S8 in the ESM). The physical properties of the catalyst after the stability test was characterized by SEM observation (Fig. S9 in the ESM). It shows that the overall hollow polyhedral morphology can be preserved well after the durability test in acid electrolyte. In addition, ICP-AES was employed to retrace the stability of Co-W phosphides in acid electrolyte after long-term stability test ( $50\mathrm{~h~}$ at $20\mathrm{\mA{\cdot}c m^{-2}},$ . The concentrations of dissolved Co, W and P are determined to be 0.1142, 0.1005 and $0.2176~{\upmu\mathrm{mmol/L}}$ , respectively (Fig. S10 in the ESM). In contrast, similar results are also obtained for the other two Co-W phosphides, where the concentration of dissolved Co is also slightly higher than that of W whilst the concentration of P is approximately the sum of Co and W concentrations, indicating the homogeneous dissolution of Co-W phosphides in acid electrolyte during the electrolysis process.
The HER property of the Co-W phosphides was also evaluated in $1.0\mathrm{M}$ KOH solution to demonstrate their versatile applications. As shown in Fig. 5(a), $\mathrm{Co_{0.9}W_{1.1}P_{2}/C}$ also exhibits the best HER activity among the ternary phosphides. To afford a current density of $10\mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , a small overpotential of $54\mathrm{mV}$ is required for $\mathrm{Co_{0.9}W_{1.1}P_{2}/C}$ electrode (Fig. 5(c)). Tafel slope of $\mathrm{Co_{0.9}W_{1.1}P_{2}/C}$ ( ${59\ \mathrm{\mV\cdotdec^{-1}}},$ ) is also close to that of $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ ( ${52\mathrm{mV}}{\cdot}{\mathrm{dec}}^{-1}, $ , which is much smaller than those of $\mathrm{Co}_{0.95}\mathrm{W}_{1.05}\mathrm{P}_{2}/\mathrm{C}$ and ${\mathrm{CoWP}}_{2}/{\mathrm{C}}_{3}$ , suggesting its superior HER kinetics in alkaline solution. It is noted that $\mathrm{Co_{0.9}W_{1.1}P_{2}/C}$ can also perform well against high durability under alkaline conditions. As illustrated in Fig. 5(d), the required voltage only experiences a slight increase when a current density of $20\mathrm{\mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ is applied. Its excellent stability can further be confirmed by LSV curve of $\mathrm{Co_{0.9}W_{1.1}P_{2}/C}$ after 50 hours’ electrocatalysis (Fig. S11 in the ESM).
As a vital step of HER process, the water dissociation on different surfaces were also calculated and illustrated in Fig. 6(f). Comparing with ${\mathrm{CoWP}}_{2}$ (001)T4, the thermodynamic barriers for water dissociation are distinctly reduced while the kinetics barriers are increased for ${\mathrm{Co}}(\mathrm{W})$ on $\scriptstyle\mathrm{Co}$ site, indicating that Co site is not the active sites for water dissociation due to the high kinetics barrier $(1.26\mathrm{eV})$ . In contrast, it is noted that the thermodynamic and kinetics barriers for water dissociation are both simultaneously reduced for $\operatorname{Co}(\mathsf{W})$ on W site, which demonstrates that W site could facilitate the water dissociation due to the optimized surrounding electronic environment.
